#X text 460 20 slot_router_stem <name>: one stem's worth of slot_router logic \, instantiated 4x (vocals/melody/bass/drums) by slot_router.pd. <name> is cosmetic only (shows in the object box) \, does not affect wiring. Inlet takes pre-tagged messages from the parent dispatcher: "prep <ringSlot> <segDurMs> <stretchRatio>" (ringSlot is intentionally ignored/dead-ended -- ring-buffer double-buffering isn't wired up yet \, see buffer_manager.js native-Pd rewrite task -- this always plays the stem's raw full buffer)\, "commit"\, "stop"\, "resume"., f 360;
#X text 460 160 KNOWN SIMPLIFICATION: no guard against commit/resume firing before any prep -- the original slot_router.js's commit() checked "no pending prepare\, ignoring" and no-op'd. Here a commit with no prior prep just multiplies two zeroed stashes (0 * ratio = 0ms delay) and sends ratio/play anyway. Low-risk since the real caller (buffer_manager\, not yet bridged) is expected to always prep before commit \, same as the original., f 300;
#X text 460 260 RESUME = COMMIT here \, deliberately: karma~ could truly pause-in-place and resume() relied on that (no re-seek\, no delay restart). stem_timestretch~ has no pause state -- "play" always restarts the segment from 0 (see stem_timestretch~.pd's own doc comment). So resuming has to mean "replay this segment from the top\, incl. restarting its auto-advance timer" -- exactly what commit already does. This is a real behavior difference from karma~\, not an oversight., f 300;
#X text 460 380 4 outlets\, left to right: 0 = "ratio <f>" message (to stem_timestretch~'s inlet)\, 1 = "play"/"stop" symbol (to stem_timestretch~'s inlet -- same inlet as outlet 0\, Pd allows two cords into one inlet)\, 2 = delayMs float = segDurMs * stretchRatio (to the per-stem [delay]'s time/cold inlet)\, 3 = bang (start the delay countdown on commit) or the message "stop" (cancel it) -- to the per-stem [delay]'s hot inlet. Outlet order is determined by these [outlet] objects' LEFT-TO-RIGHT X POSITION (Pd convention)\, not file order -- kept explicitly ascending (x=40/140/240/340) to avoid that trap., f 380;
